  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes viral strategies for mapping neural connections, organized by tracing direction and how far the label spreads. The top row shows anterograde tracing, following signals from an injected neuron toward downstream partners. A circuit-specific approach uses two fluorescent AAV labels to mark projections to selected target regions. A monosynaptic method with AAV1 appears to move one synapse forward, labeling immediate postsynaptic target cell types. A polysynaptic approach using HSV-H129 spreads across multiple downstream neurons, illustrating stepwise transmission through a chain of connected cells.\n\nThe bottom row shows retrograde tracing, moving from axon terminals back to upstream neurons. A circuit-specific method uses viruses such as PRV, rabies, HSV, AAVrg, or CAV to label neurons projecting into a target region. A monosynaptic retrograde strategy combines helper AAVs with EnvA-pseudotyped rabies, restricting spread to a defined starter cell type and then to its direct presynaptic inputs. A polysynaptic retrograde method with PRV-Bartha spreads backward through several connected neurons, revealing broader upstream cell-type networks.\n\nOverall, the figure compares directionality, synaptic specificity, and the kinds of targets each viral tracing method can reveal.",
